  polymorphic — a discriminated union on event type is the thing that keeps the fan-out logic honest. The realistic
  alternative is Python + FastAPI + SQLModel, which is equally good and worth taking if you're more fluent there. A SPA
  framework (React etc.) I'd skip: an inbox and a task list are server-rendered pages with a polling badge, and a build
  pipeline buys nothing yet.

  [1mAuth.[0m Multi-user forces identity. I recommend [1memail + password with server-side sessions[0m (httpOnly cookie, hashed with
  argon2/bcrypt) — no third-party dependency, and we need to store email addresses anyway for the later email channel,
  so it's not extra work. OAuth is fewer security decisions but adds a provider dependency and still needs local user
  rows.

  [1mTooling to set up from the start[0m — the skill asks explicitly, and these land in the spec's Global Constraints so every
  later task inherits them:

  - [1mLinting + auto-format[0m — ESLint + Prettier. Recommend yes; near-zero cost now, annoying to retrofit.
  - [1mUnit test infrastructure[0m — runner, layout, one passing fixture. Recommend yes, emphatically: fan-out and
    subscription rules are exactly the logic that needs tests, and TDD is the workflow we'd use for them.
  - [1mEnd-to-end tests[0m — Playwright over the real pages. Recommend yes but [3mthin[0m: one happy path (user A comments, user B
    sees a badge) is worth a lot here because it's the only thing that proves the whole chain end to end. Not a broad
    suite.
  - [1mFuzz / mutation testing[0m — recommend skipping. Low value for CRUD-and-fan-out logic; revisit if the subscription
    rules get genuinely intricate.
